          <h3 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-10 mb-4">Why the Timeline Costs More Than the Materials</h3>

          <p>
            Sealing is cheap in materials and expensive in elapsed time. A realistic schedule runs across three days: clean and wash on the first, fill cracks and let them cure on the second, then two coats of sealer on the third with 24 to 48 hours before any vehicle returns. Add the days needed for weed die-off if anything is growing in the cracks, and the project spans most of a week even though the actual work is a handful of hours.
          </p>

          <p>
            That timeline is the reason the weather window matters so much, and the reason buying an extra pail up front is worth doing. A mid-project trip to the store is not merely inconvenient — it can push the second coat past the point where the forecast still supports it, which means either an unbalanced one-coat-and-two-coat driveway or waiting for the next settled spell entirely.
          </p>

          <p>
            Set against professional quotes, the DIY materials for a standard two-car driveway are a fraction of the cost. What you are trading is a week of scheduling and a weekend of physical work. For a sound driveway that only needs this every two to three years, most homeowners find that trade clearly worthwhile.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            7. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying for One Coat</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                The coverage figure on the pail almost always describes a single coat, and almost every driveway needs two. This is the most common and most expensive planning error, because it is usually discovered partway through the second coat when the store is closed and the weather window is closing.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Stretching a Pail Beyond Its Coverage Range</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Spreading sealer thinner than roughly a gallon per 100 square feet does not save money. It produces a film too thin to survive freeze-thaw cycling, which brings the next sealing job forward by a year or more. The coverage range is a specification for how the product is designed to perform.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Estimating the Area Instead of Measuring It</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Driveways are consistently larger than they look, and the turning apron in front of the garage is routinely left out of a mental estimate. Five minutes with a tape measure costs nothing and prevents both underbuying and the opposite error of buying two pails more than you need.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Forgetting Crack Filler and Primer</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Sealer is the biggest line item but it is not the only one. Crack filler, backer rod for deep cracks, degreaser and oil spot primer all need to be on hand before you start, because each has its own cure time that has to happen before sealer goes down.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Assuming All Pails Are the Same Size</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Driveway sealer is sold in 4.75-gallon and 5-gallon pails depending on brand, and some products come in different concentrations. Check the actual volume and the stated coverage on the specific product you are buying rather than assuming a standard pail.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much driveway sealer do I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Plan on roughly one gallon per 80 to 100 square feet per coat, depending on how porous your asphalt is, and multiply by two coats. A standard 600 square foot two-car driveway needs about three 4.75-gallon pails on an average surface and four on a heavily weathered one.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many square feet does a 5 gallon bucket of driveway sealer cover?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Roughly 400 to 500 square feet for a single coat, depending on surface porosity. A smooth, recently sealed driveway sits at the higher end; grey, coarse asphalt with visible aggregate sits at the lower end. Remember that figure is per coat, and most driveways need two.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How do I calculate driveway sealer for an irregular driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Break it into rectangles, calculate each separately and add the totals. A driveway with a turning apron in front of the garage is usually two rectangles. For curved entries, measure the widest points and accept a small overestimate rather than trying to calculate the curve exactly.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is it better to buy too much or too little driveway sealer?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Too much, without question. An unopened pail can usually be returned, whereas running out partway through a coat leaves a visible boundary that persists for the life of the job, and the trip to buy more can push you outside the dry weather window the sealer needs to cure.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does surface condition change how much sealer I need?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Significantly. Porous, weathered asphalt with visible aggregate absorbs a substantial part of the first coat into surface voids, consuming roughly 80 square feet per gallon against 100 for a smooth surface. On a large driveway that difference alone can be a whole extra pail.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How much does it cost to seal a driveway yourself?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                The sealer is the main cost — typically three to four pails for a standard two-car driveway on a two-coat job. Add crack filler, oil spot primer and an applicator if you do not own one. Use the calculator above with your local pail price for a figure specific to your driveway.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">How many pails of sealer for a 2 car driveway?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                A standard two-car driveway of roughly 600 square feet needs about three 4.75-gallon pails for two coats on an average surface, or four if the asphalt is grey, coarse and porous. Measure rather than assume — the turning apron in front of the garage is routinely left out of estimates and can add a hundred square feet.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Should I buy sealer by the pail or in bulk?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Pails are the practical choice for a residential driveway. Bulk quantities only make sense for very large areas, and once opened, sealer has a limited shelf life and cannot be stored reliably from one season to the next. Buy what the calculator indicates plus one pail of margin.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does the calculator account for two coats?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Yes. Select the number of coats and the calculator multiplies the area accordingly, then adds a ten percent allowance for edging, waste and the residue left in each pail. Two coats is the recommended default for almost every driveway.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

