        <!-- Closing -->
        <div class="mt-16 bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8">
          <h2 class="text-2xl font-black text-white mb-4">The Bottom Line</h2>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The arithmetic that matters is simple: your square footage, divided by 80 to 100 depending on how porous the surface is, multiplied by two coats, plus about ten percent for edging and waste. That is your gallon figure, and dividing by 4.75 gives you pails.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            The error almost everyone makes is treating the coverage number on the pail as the whole job. It describes one coat on a favourable surface. Two coats on weathered asphalt can need close to double what a quick reading of the label suggests, and discovering that partway through the second coat is how driveways end up with a permanent visible seam.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            Buy the extra pail. It is the cheapest insurance in the project, it is usually returnable unopened, and it removes the one scenario that genuinely ruins a sealing job — stopping halfway because the weather window closed while you were at the store.
          </p>
          <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-4">
            One last note on storage: sealer does not keep well once a pail has been opened, and it does not survive freezing. If you finish with a partial pail, use it on a garden path or a shed apron rather than planning to store it for next season, and return anything still sealed.
          </p>
